# `()` as an `extern` PARAMETER is exit 1 on the author's line, and was exit 2 —
# the compiler blaming itself for a declaration the author wrote.
#
# SYMPTOM (measured 2026-08-16): `extern function abs(n: ()) -> i32` passed
# `heroes check` at exit 0, and `heroes build` then said "internal error:
# compiling the generated C failed" over
# `_Static_assert(HERO_RET_I32(abs((void *)0)), …)` — incompatible pointer to
# integer conversion. Every other way to get an `extern` signature wrong is exit 1
# on the `.hero` line; this one was the compiler's fault by §7's reading, and it
# was not.
#
# CAUSE: `ffi_decls::crosses_the_boundary` is shared by results and parameters,
# and `()` is a legal RESULT — it is how a `void` function is declared. So the
# signature check passed, and `zero_of` reached its `Ty::Unit` arm with no C
# spelling to write. That arm's own comment had recorded the defect and named the
# repair as a frontend refusal; this is the refusal.
#
# WHY THE FRONTEND CAN DECIDE IT, which is the test for whether a refusal belongs
# there at all: it is a fact about C's GRAMMAR, not about any particular header. A
# parameter list has no spelling for an argument of type `void`, so no header
# anywhere can take one, so there is nothing to read a header for. Contrast `ptr`
# and `cstr`, which are ordinary C parameter types that may or may not match a
# given header — only clang can answer those, and it does.
#
# The `()` RESULT below is in the file on purpose: it is the shape that must keep
# working, and a refusal that broke it would be worse than the defect.
# Date: 2026-08-16.

extern "stdlib.h"
    function abs(n: ()) -> i32      #~ ffi_parameter_type
    function free(p: ptr)

function main()
    print(1)
